---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/docs/Callout.astro';
import Code from '../../components/docs/Code.astro';
import { page } from '../../data/docs-nav';
import { COUNTS, REQUIREMENTS, spell } from '../../data/docs';

const entry = page('/docs/getting-started/');

const build = `git clone https://github.com/thatcube/hozz.git
cd hozz

# Signing lives in a gitignored Local.xcconfig:
echo "DEVELOPMENT_TEAM = YOURTEAMID" > Local.xcconfig

# Build and install on a connected iPhone:
tools/device-build.sh`;
---

<DocsLayout page={entry}>
  <p>
    The iPhone app reads Apple Health. The optional Mac app receives, charts and serves that data to
    assistants.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Install it</h2>

  <p>
    Hozz is early alpha and <strong>not in the App Store</strong>. Build it with Xcode {
      REQUIREMENTS.xcode
    }+ and{' '}
    <a href="https://github.com/yonaskolb/XcodeGen">XcodeGen</a> {REQUIREMENTS.xcodegen} or newer.
  </p>

  <Code caption="Terminal" code={build} />

  <p><code>tools/mac-build.sh</code> builds the Mac app.</p>

  <Callout kind="note" title="One thing that will bite you on a device build">
    <p>
      Device builds need the App Group <code>group.com.thatcube.Hozz</code> in your developer
      profile. Simulator builds do not.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>2. Give it Health access</h2>

  <p>
    Grant any subset. Change it later in{' '}
    <strong>Settings → Health → Data Access &amp; Devices → Hozz</strong>.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="warn" title="Hozz cannot tell “denied” from “you have none”">
    <p>
      HealthKit reports both cases identically. If expected data is missing, check Health settings.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>3. Pick a destination</h2>

  <p>
    Nothing leaves until you add and confirm a destination.
  </p>

  <p>
    On <strong>Automatic</strong>, choose a discovered Mac or one of {spell(COUNTS.presets)} presets.
    Start with <a href="/docs/destinations/folder/">Folder</a> if unsure.
  </p>

  <p>
    Choose its Health types, format and cadence. <strong>Send a test</strong> uses a probe with no
    Health data.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Run the first export</h2>

  <h3>Automatic sync</h3>

  <p>
    Automatic sync works through history in bounded batches, sharing each pass across selected types.
  </p>

  <p>
    The phone reports completed types; the Mac reports received types and date coverage. Health does
    not reveal totals before reading them, so Hozz cannot give a percentage or estimate.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="note" title="A first backfill looks slow because it is">
    <p>
      iOS grants short background windows. Large archives can take days or weeks.{' '}
      <a href="/docs/troubleshooting/">Tell slow from stuck.</a>
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h3>Manual export</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Export</strong> builds a historical NDJSON, CSV, JSON, SQLite, Markdown or GPX file.
    Interrupted exports resume from their last durable checkpoint.
  </p>

  <p>Manual export is independent of automatic destinations.</p>
</DocsLayout>
